-- title:
--   ℤ₍ₚ₎ as the localisation of ℤ at (p)
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a natural number, assumed prime, and suppose the ideal of $\mathbb{Z}$ generated by $p$ is prime. Let [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt p`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) be the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ whose elements are the rationals $q$ whose denominator $q.\mathrm{den}$ (the positive denominator of the reduced representative) is coprime to $p$; the subring structure records that this set contains $0$ and $1$ and is closed under negation, addition and multiplication, using that the denominator of a sum or product divides the product of the denominators. The theorem asserts `IsLocalization.AtPrime` for this subring, with its canonical $\mathbb{Z}$-algebra structure, with respect to the prime ideal $(p)\subset\mathbb{Z}$: the structure map $\mathbb{Z}\to\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ sends every integer outside $(p)$, i.e. every integer not divisible by $p$, to a unit; every element of the subring is of the form $a/s$ with $a\in\mathbb{Z}$ and $s\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus(p)$; and two integers with the same image, after multiplication by some element of $\mathbb{Z}\setminus(p)$, become equal (here, being equal outright).
--
--   This is the elementary identification of the ring $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ of rationals with denominator prime to $p$ as the localisation of $\mathbb{Z}$ at the prime ideal $(p)$, which makes Mathlib's localisation and local-ring API available for this subring of $\mathbb{Q}$. It is used throughout the integral-model and flatness parts of the development, for instance when base-changing two-chart integral models of curves to $\mathbb{Z}_{(p)}$ or analysing reducedness and connectedness of the resulting fibres.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_GaloisRep_isLocalization_ratLocalizedAt.lean

import Definitions.Def_GaloisRep_Flat

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
